# Author : Krishnendu De
# Co-Author: Claude Fable 5.0
#
# Worked examples. This file is named `.disabled` ON PURPOSE: `load_actions` globs
# *.yml / *.yaml only, so nothing here is live. Copy the rule you want into a real
# `.yaml` file, adjust the logsource to your estate, and check /health afterwards.
#
# Every rule below is a complete, valid document — `contentpack`-style strictness
# applies at load time, so a typo is a load error you will see, not a silent no-op.

# ── 1. Mask an account name on a noisy source ────────────────────────────────
# `scrub` (on by default) is the part that matters: masking only the named column is
# theatre, because the value is still in `raw` (stored verbatim as jsonb and
# GIN-indexed) and in whatever sibling column it was extracted alongside — usually
# `message`, which normalize.tsv_text feeds to the full-text vector separately. The
# sweep covers raw recursively and the other text columns, so the value is actually
# gone rather than hidden from one view of it.
id: mask-web-proxy-users
title: Pseudonymise usernames on web proxy logs
description: >
  Works-council / GDPR posture: the proxy logs are kept for security analysis but the
  acting account is pseudonymised, so "same user" analysis still works and the identity
  does not.
enabled: false
order: 10
logsource:
  log_type: proxy
action: mask
mask:
  fields: [user_name]
  mode: hash          # deterministic: the same user always maps to the same token
  salt: change-me     # rotate to break the mapping; keep secret or the hash is reversible
  scrub: true

---
# ── 2. Sample a high-volume, low-value flow source ───────────────────────────
# `key` is what keeps this honest: the bucket is derived from the named columns, so one
# host is kept or dropped WHOLE. Sampling per event instead would give you 10% of what
# every host did, which answers no question anyone actually asks.
id: sample-netflow-noise
title: Keep 1 flow in 10 from the lab exporter
description: >
  The lab segment exports more flow than it is worth. Sampled by src_ip so a host's
  history is either complete or absent, never a tenth of itself.
enabled: false
order: 50
logsource:
  vendor: netflow
detection:
  lab:
    src_ip|cidr: 10.99.0.0/16
  condition: lab
action: sample
sample:
  rate: 10
  key: [src_ip]

---
# ── 3. Drop a known-benign health check ──────────────────────────────────────
# COMMENTED OUT, and that is the point: a `drop` is unrecoverable — the event is never
# written, so there is nothing to un-drop later. Prove the condition matches only what
# you think it does (search for it first, or run it as a `route` for a day) before you
# enable one. Note this rule is narrow on THREE terms; a drop keyed on one field is how
# you lose a source.
#
# id: drop-loadbalancer-healthcheck
# title: Drop the load-balancer health probe
# enabled: false
# order: 90
# logsource:
#   log_type: access
# detection:
#   probe:
#     src_ip: 10.0.0.4
#     url|startswith: /healthz
#     status: 200
#   condition: probe
# action: drop
